      "id": "1559180815269757009",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-10-12T12:27:54.539+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": "2026-10-12T12:32:14.066+00:00",
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "To share my personal experience: I taped out a RISC-V core on GFMPW-1, about as performant as a non-pipelined core could get, and wired it up to a SPI <-> 10/100mbps ethernet bridge (enc28j60) and the system kept dropping packets because the RISC-V CPU struggled to process them faster than they were coming in.",
      "author": {
        "id": "220639106915368960",
        "name": "tholin",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Tholin",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-10_media/edae5aadf146133e0448bfb1f0fa91c4-62B19.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
      "reactions": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1559181190253121547",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-10-12T12:29:23.942+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": "2026-10-12T12:31:01.609+00:00",
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Like, it’d frequently die during IPv6 SLAAC or IPv4 DHCP because the broadcast responses combined with background broadcast traffic on my home network overflowed the buffer.",
      "author": {
        "id": "220639106915368960",
        "name": "tholin",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Tholin",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-10_media/edae5aadf146133e0448bfb1f0fa91c4-62B19.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
      "reactions": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
